all right hello everyone my name is  sally we're here at cube con barcelona  and with me today is aaron creek and  berger he has a lot of hats that he  wears  he's a Google engineer he is on the  kubernetes steering committee and he's  been involved with a lot of kubernetes  releases aaron can you talk a little bit  about the releases that you've been  involved with and how they've evolved  over time sure I've been involved with  kubernetes releases since kubernetes was  at version 1.4 and at the beginning it  was just sort of a select few google  people who were hardcore enough and had  the right privileges to make this happen  and over time I saw the release process  gradually handover more and more control  to the community  I started out writing release notes you  know attempting to sort of reverse  engineer a coherent narrative out of  this chaotic open source process and  over time grew into other roles such as  you know issue triage keeping track of  how do we actually know what's happening  within the release and make sure the  right things are being focused on to to  see I signal you know how do we make  sure that a given kubernetes release is  actually healthy and ready to ship out  the door and that sort of culminated in  me acting as a release lead for  kubernetes 1:14 which I was especially  proud of because it was the the release  with the most stable enhancements out of  any kubernetes release and it was also  the first release where the community  agreed to use the kubernetes enhancement  proposal process or cap process to to  ship features which to me was really a  sign of just how mature the release  process has come and while I was doing  this I was assisted by a team of over 40  people serving in 13 different roles to  ensure that mostly all I had to do was  watch you know everything proceed super  smoothly and it culminated in me getting  to create this this cat t-shirt here  where every release team member gets a  piece of swag as sort of you know the  honorary badge of running the release  for three months and my piece of swag  was was quatre Nettie's you know three  cats looking up at the kubernetes logo  sort of like three Wolf Moon very cool  and so you mentioned how this process  has become better and  is more community owned there's a lot of  other work going on to improve the  kubernetes community can you talk about  some of that sure  back when kubernetes first started we we  weren't even sort of very organized in  terms of what we were doing it was just  some very driven companies and  contributors trying to push code out as  quickly as possible and we gradually  realized that we needed to get  like-minded people together to talk  about different areas that they were  interested in coordinating on so I used  to work with a guy named Bob Boyce who I  worked with him to co-found seek  scalability the first special interest  group I then worked to co-found the  second special interest group called  seek testing all about the testing of  kubernetes to make sure that that worked  well today we now have over 31 special  interest groups and we've also created  working groups that are collaborations  between special interest groups so we  fairly sort of federated out and  empowered the decision-making process  for this open source community and at  the same time we've been very  intentional about focusing on inclusion  and diversity through this whole process  taking a look at how we engage with our  with our community members and with our  contributors and looking at the examples  that we set to ensure that we're acting  and as inclusive a manner as possible  sort of setting up work and describing  roles to make it easier to onboard  people who are maybe intimidated or  overwhelmed by contributing to a project  that is it was large and fast moving as  kubernetes so i've really been happy to  see that that sort of payoff and I think  that attitude has been reflected in in  the keuken conference you know 